Myth: Before the hills had shadows, the world lay sealed beneath a shell of gray stone. Above it swam Nara, the moon-horned tortoise, carrying night like a cloak upon her back. She heard no laughter, no leaves, no wingbeats—only the silence of sleeping earth.

Nara wished to wake the world, so she lowered her head and struck the stone with one shining horn. A crack opened, and from it rose a single clear spring. But the spring trembled, afraid to travel.

Then Nara called three wandering stars. She set one in the water, and it became a fish to guide the current. She set one on the shore, and it became reeds to whisper courage. She set the last in the air, and it became a blue bird to sing the road ahead.

The spring followed the song, curling into a river. Wherever it passed, soil softened, seeds opened, and animals lifted their faces to drink. Nara smiled, but warned, “Water is a gift that lives only when shared.”

So people still place bright stones beside rivers in thanks. At dawn, when reeds murmur and birds call, they say Nara is listening to see whether the world remembers.
